# #Built-in types
# ##Truth value testing##Built-in types
# By default, an object is considered true unless its class defines either a __bool__() method that
# returns False or a __len__() method that returns zero, when called with the object. Here are most
# of the built-in objects considered false:
# - constants defined to be false: None and False.
# - zero of any numeric type: 0, 0.0, 0j, Decimal(0), Fraction(0, 1)
# - empty sequences and collections: '', (), [], {}, set(), range(0)
# Operations and built-in functions that have a Boolean result always return 0 or False for false
# and 1 or True for true, unless otherwise stated. (Important exception: the Boolean operations or
# and and always return one of their operands.)
# ###Boolean operations##boolean#Built-in types
# - `and` : x or y : this is a short-circuit operator, so it only evaluates the second argument if
#   the first one is false.
# - `or` : x and y : this is a short-circuit operator, so it only evaluates the second argument if
#   the first one is true
# - `not` : not x : not has a lower priority than non-Boolean operators, so not a == b is
#   interpreted as not (a == b), and a == not b is a syntax error.
# ##Comparisons#Built-in types
# There are eight comparison operations in Python. They all have the same priority (which is higher
# than that of the Boolean operations). Comparisons can be chained arbitrarily; for example, x < y
# <= z is equivalent to x < y and y <= z, except that y is evaluated only once (but in both cases z
# is not evaluated at all when x < y is found to be false)
# - `<` strictly less than
# - `<=` less than or equal
# - `>` strictly greater than
# - `>=` greater than or equal
# - `==` equal
# - `!=` not equal
# - `is` object identity
# - `is not` negated object identity
# Objects of different types, except different numeric types, never compare equal. Furthermore, some
# types (for example, function objects) support only a degenerate notion of comparison where any two
# objects of that type are unequal. The <, <=, > and >= operators raise a TypeError exception when
# comparing a complex number with another built-in numeric type, when the objects are of different
# types that cannot be compared, or in other cases where there is no defined ordering.
# Non-identical instances of a class normally compare as non-equal unless the class defines the
# __eq__() method.
# Instances of a class cannot be ordered with respect to other instances of the same class, or other
# types of object, unless the class defines enough of the methods __lt__(), __le__(), __gt__(), and
# __ge__() (in general, __lt__() and __eq__() are sufficient if you want the conventional meanings
# of the comparison operators).
# The is and is not operators cannot be customized; also they can be applied to any two objects and
# never raise an exception.
# Two more operations with the same syntactic priority, in and not in, are supported by types that
# are iterable or implement the __contains__() method.
# ##Numeric types#Built-in types
# ###int##Numeric types#Built-in types
# ```python
# n: int = -37
# bin(n) # -0b100101
# n.bit_length() # 6 :  if n is non-zero, n.bit_length() is the unique positive integer k such that
# 2**(k-1) <= abs(n) < 2**k
# def bit_length(self):
#     s = bin(self)       # binary representation:  bin(-37) --> '-0b100101'
#     s = s.lstrip('-0b') # remove leading zeros and minus sign
#     return len(s)       # len('100101') --> 6
# ```
# ###bool (subtype of int)##Numeric types#Built-in types
# ```python
# # float.as_integer_ratio() returns a pair of integers whose ratio is exactly equal to the original
# float, with a positive denominator. Raises OverflowError on infinities and ValueError on NaNs.
# # float.is_integer() returns True if the float instance is finite with an integral value, and
# False otherwise
# print((-2.0).is_integer()) # True
# print((3.2).is_integer()) # False
# # float.hex() returns a representation of a floating-point number as a hexadecimal string. For
# finite floats, this representation always includes a leading 0x and a trailing p and exponent
# # classmethod float.fromhex(s) class method that returns the float represented by the hexadecimal
# string s. The string s may have leading and trailing whitespace.
# # Note that float.hex() is an instance method, while float.fromhex() is a class method.
# print(float.fromhex('0x3.a7p10')) # 3740.0
# print(float.hex(3740.0)) # 0x1.d380000000000p+11
# ```
# ###float##Numeric types#Built-in types
# ###complex (complex numbers)##Numeric types#Built-in types
# complex(re, im) a complex number with real part re and imaginary part im. im defaults to 0.

# ##str#Built-in types
# Strings implement all of the common sequence operations, plus the additional methods listed below,
# i.e. they support the operations in ###Common operations##Sequence types#Built-in types
# ###Meaning of u,r,b,f##str#Built-in types
# - f : formatted string literals are prefixed with "f", similar to str.format(). They contain
#   replacement fields surrounded by curly braces. Replacement fields are expressions evaluated at
#   run time and then formatted using the format() protocol.
# - u : the following string is encoded as Unicode, usually used before non-ASCII (e.g. Chinese)
#   strings to avoid garbled text caused by the source file encoding
# - r : declares the following string as a raw string, i.e. \n \t etc. are not escaped
# - b : represents the bytes class
# ###str formatting##str#Built-in types
# ```python
# # {%}[(mapping key)][conversion flags][minimum field width][precision][length modifier]{conversion
# type} [] : optional {} : required
# print('%(language)s has %(number)03d quote types.' % {'language': "Python", "number": 2}) # Python
# has 002 quote types.
# print("hello %s world " % ("ldz"))
# # Formatting with f"": when a conversion is specified, the result of evaluating the expression is
# converted before formatting. Conversion '!s' calls str() on the result, '!r' calls repr(), and
# '!a' calls ascii()
# name="Fred"
# print(f"He said his name is {name!r}.") # output : He said his name is 'Fred'.
# width,precision,value = 10,4,decimal.Decimal("12.34567")
# print(f"result: {value:{width}.{precision}}") # nested fields output: result:      12.35
# today = datetime(year=2017, month=1, day=27)
# print(f"{today:%B %d, %Y}") # output: January 27, 2017
# number = 1024
# print(f"{number:#0x}") # output: 0x400
# foo,line = "bar","The mill's closed"
# print(f"{line = }") # output: line = "The mill\'s closed"
# print(f"{line = :20}") # output: "line = The mill's closed   "
# print(f"{line = !r:20}") # output: 'line = "The mill\'s closed" '
# 
# list=["hello"]
# print("{} {}".format("hello", "world"))
# print("{1[0]} {0}".format("world", list))
# print("hello {name}".format(name = "ldz"))
# # Fill and alignment
# # ^, <, > mean centered, left-aligned and right-aligned respectively, followed by the width
# # the character after : is the fill character, only one character; defaults to a space if not
# given
# print '{:>8}'.format('3.14')
# print '{:<8}'.format('3.14')
# print '{:^8}'.format('3.14')
# print '{:0>8}'.format('3.14')
# print '{:a>8}'.format('3.14')
# # Float precision
# print '{:.4f}'.format(3.1415926)
# print '{:0>10.4f}'.format(3.1415926)
# # Number bases
# # b, d, o, x mean binary, decimal, octal and hexadecimal respectively
# print '{:b}'.format(11)
# print '{:d}'.format(11)
# print '{:o}'.format(11)
# print '{:x}'.format(11)
# print '{:#x}'.format(11)
# print '{:#X}'.format(11)
# # Thousands separator
# print '{:,}'.format(15700000000)
# ```
# ##Binary sequence types#Built-in types
# The core built-in types for manipulating binary data are `bytes` and `bytearray`. They are
# supported by `memoryview`, which uses the buffer protocol to access the memory of other binary
# objects without needing to make a copy.
# ###bytes##Binary sequence types#Built-in types
# bytes objects are immutable sequences of single bytes. Since many major binary protocols are based
# on the ASCII text encoding, bytes objects offer several methods that are only valid when working
# with ASCII compatible data and are closely related to string objects in a variety of other ways.
# (i.e. `bytes_var[0] = 104` raises an error)
# ####Literals###bytes##Binary sequence types
# First, the syntax for bytes literals is largely the same as that for string literals, except that
# a b prefix is added : b"hello world python"
# While bytes literals and representations are based on ASCII text, bytes objects actually behave
# like immutable sequences of integers, with each value in the sequence restricted such that 0 <= x
# < 256 (attempts to violate this restriction will trigger ValueError).
# ###bytearray##Binary sequence types
# bytearray objects are a mutable counterpart to bytes objects.
# There is no dedicated literal syntax for bytearray objects; instead they are always created by
# calling the constructor
# - creating an empty instance: bytearray()
# - creating a zero-filled instance with a given length: bytearray(10)
# - from an iterable of integers: bytearray(range(20))
# - copying existing binary data via the buffer protocol: bytearray(b'Hi!')
# ###Memory views##Binary sequence types
# memoryview objects allow Python code to access the internal data of an object that supports the
# [buffer protocol](https://docs.python.org/zh-cn/3.11/c-api/buffer.html#bufferobjects) (bytes and
# bytearray among the built-in types) without copying.
# ```python
# import array
# a = array.array('l', [-11111111, 22222222, -33333333, 44444444])
# m = memoryview(a)
# m[0]
# -11111111
# ```
